---
import Article from "../layouts/Article.astro";
import { go } from "../data/site";
const toc = [
  { id: "available", label: "Availability" },
  { id: "signup", label: "Sign up and log in" },
  { id: "payments", label: "Payment methods" },
  { id: "withdraw", label: "Withdrawals" },
  { id: "legal", label: "Rules and risks" },
  { id: "faq", label: "FAQ" },
];
const schema = [{ "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", mainEntity: [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I deposit in naira?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Bank transfer, naira cards (Visa, Mastercard, Verve), OPay and PalmPay are listed for NGN. The minimum deposit is $5 for most methods."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is Pocket Option legit in Nigeria?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It has operated since 2017, but it is not registered with SEC Nigeria, so there is no local investor protection."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How long do naira withdrawals take?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "After verification, local transfers usually arrive the same day to a few days, depending on the bank."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does Pocket Option work on mobile in Nigeria?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Use the Android app, the iPhone web app or any mobile browser."}}] }];
---

<Article
  cover="deposit-withdrawal"
  title="Pocket Option Nigeria: Naira Deposits, OPay & PalmPay"
  description="Pocket Option in Nigeria: sign up and log in, deposit from $5 in naira by bank transfer, Verve card, OPay or PalmPay, withdraw and use the app safely."
  h1="Pocket Option in Nigeria"
  lead="How Nigerian traders fund Pocket Option in naira, get verified and withdraw, with the regulatory points to know before you start."
  crumb="Nigeria"
  toc={toc}
  schema={schema}
>
  <h2 id="available">Is Pocket Option available in Nigeria?</h2>
  <p>Nigeria is not on Pocket Option's restricted list, and Nigerian accounts can deposit in naira through local banks, cards and fintech wallets. Account currency can be US dollars; local payments are converted from NGN (Nigerian naira) at checkout.</p>

  <h2 id="signup">Sign up and log in</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Pocket Option and register with your email or Google account.</li>
    <li>Confirm your email, then try the <a href="/demo-account/">free $50,000 demo</a> before depositing.</li>
    <li>Complete verification in your profile with an ID that matches your name. It is required before your first withdrawal.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Most Nigerian traders use the Android app. If the site is slow on mobile data, switch to the web platform's lighter mobile view or use Wi-Fi for deposits. See the <a href="/app/">app guide</a> for install steps.</p>
  <p>
    <a href={go("register")} rel="sponsored nofollow">Open a Pocket Option account →</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="payments">Payment methods in Nigeria</h2>
  <p>These local methods are listed on Pocket Option's official payment page, all with 0% Pocket Option commission. The minimum deposit is $5 for most methods. What you see at checkout can vary by account.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Method</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>How it works</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Bank Transfer (NGN)</td>
        <td>Bank</td>
        <td>Transfer naira from your Nigerian bank account to the account shown at checkout.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Visa, Mastercard, Verve (NGN)</td>
        <td>Card</td>
        <td>Pay with a naira card, including Verve.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>OPay (NGN)</td>
        <td>Bank</td>
        <td>Pay from your OPay account.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>PalmPay (NGN)</td>
        <td>Bank</td>
        <td>Pay from your PalmPay account.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Cards, crypto such as USDT and Bitcoin, and e-wallets like Jeton and Volet are also available worldwide.</p>
  <h3>Deposit tips</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>When paying by bank transfer, send the exact amount shown and use the account details generated for that payment only.</li>
    <li>Card payments may need online authorisation from your bank; enable international or online payments if a card is declined.</li>
    <li>Withdraw to a bank account or wallet in your own name that you used to deposit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="withdraw">Withdrawals</h2>
  <p>Open <strong>Finance → Withdrawal</strong> after your account is verified. Anti-money-laundering rules require funds to go back to a method you used to deposit. Pocket Option sets no minimum withdrawal when no deposit bonus is active. Full details are on the <a href="/deposit-withdrawal/">deposits and withdrawals page</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="legal">Rules and risks in Nigeria</h2>
  <p>Pocket Option is not registered with Nigeria's Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC Nigeria), which regularly warns about unregistered online investment platforms. Treat it as an offshore broker without local protection.</p>
  <p>Short-term trading carries a high risk of loss. Only trade money you can afford to lose, and read our <a href="/review/">Pocket Option review</a> before opening a real account.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">Pocket Option Nigeria FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Can I deposit in naira?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Bank transfer, naira cards (Visa, Mastercard, Verve), OPay and PalmPay are listed for NGN. The minimum deposit is $5 for most methods.</p>
  <h3>Is Pocket Option legit in Nigeria?</h3>
  <p>It has operated since 2017, but it is not registered with SEC Nigeria, so there is no local investor protection.</p>
  <h3>How long do naira withdrawals take?</h3>
  <p>After verification, local transfers usually arrive the same day to a few days, depending on the bank.</p>
  <h3>Does Pocket Option work on mobile in Nigeria?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Use the Android app, the iPhone web app or any mobile browser.</p>

  <p>Other countries: <a href="/india/">Pocket Option India</a> · <a href="/pakistan/">Pocket Option Pakistan</a> · <a href="/bangladesh/">Pocket Option Bangladesh</a></p>
</Article>
